What is EFK?

  EFK stands for:

    E → Elasticsearch
    F → Fluent Bit (or Fluentd)
    K → Kibana

It is a centralized logging stack.
Instead of SSH into every server or pod to read log files, all logs are collected in one place where you can search, filter, and visualize them.


Why do we need EFK?
    Imagine your Java application runs in Kubernetes.
Pod-1
Pod-2
Pod-3
Pod-4

Each pod produces logs.

Example:

INFO User Login
INFO Order Created
ERROR Database Timeout
WARN Cache Miss

If Pod-2 crashes, Kubernetes creates a new pod.

Pod-2 (Deleted)
↓
Pod-5 (New)

The old pod's logs disappear unless you collected them elsewhere.
This is why Kubernetes logging should be centralized.



Without EFK:
Suppose you have 50 pods.
kubectl logs pod1
kubectl logs pod2
kubectl logs pod3
kubectl logs pod4
...

Finding one error across dozens of pods is tedious.


With EFK:
All Pods
    │
    ▼
Fluent Bit
    │
    ▼
Elasticsearch
    │
    ▼
Kibana

Now you open Kibana and search:
  ERROR or Exception or CustomerID=1023

and see matching logs from every pod in one place.


How Kubernetes Stores Logs?

Your Java application writes to standard output:
System.out.println("Application Started");
or
logger.info("User Login");

Inside the container, these go to stdout/stderr.

The container runtime writes them to log files on the node.

For example:
/var/log/containers/
/var/log/pods/

You generally don't read these directly; Kubernetes tooling and log collectors do.



Problem Statement:
Suppose you have:
  Java App
  Node.js App
  Python App
  NGINX
  MySQL
  MongoDB

Each application generates logs independently.
Without centralized logging, you have to inspect each workload separately.



Solution:
    Java App
    Node App
    Python App
    NGINX

      │

      ▼
  Fluent Bit (DaemonSet)

      │

      ▼
  Elasticsearch (StatefulSet)

      │

      ▼
  Kibana (Deployment/SVC)

One dashboard for everything.  
